Q: CS50 substitution - Loop not working for length of plaintext A: <p>In analyzing your issue, it is a bit hard to derive the definitive issue as there is a lack of closing blocks and possibly some other code.  So, going with that and using a bit of artistic license, following is a refactored version of the code block, filling in some missing pieces and simplifying the testing and character replacement.</p>
<pre><code>    string plain = get_string(&quot;plaintext: &quot;);//prompt user for text

    printf(&quot;ciphertext: &quot;);

    int lenp = strlen(plain);//get length of text

    int c;  /* Eliminated the pointer usage */

    for (int q = 0; q &lt; lenp; q++)
    {
        if (isalpha(plain[q]))
        {
            if (isupper(plain[q]))
            {
                c = toupper(argv[1][plain[q] - 'A']);   /* Handle uppercase */
            }
            else
            {
                c = argv[1][plain[q] - 'a'];        /* Handle lowercase */
            }
        }
        else
        {
            c = plain[q];               /* Handle non-alpha */
        }

        printf(&quot;%c&quot;, c);
    }

    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Following, is an explanation of the refactoring:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Since each character within the entered character array would be individually processed, there was no need for any address pointers, so those variables were eliminated.</p>
</li>
<li><p>In the original code, there was only accommodation for uppercase letter processing, so the &quot;if&quot; block was enhanced into an &quot;if/else&quot; block using character functions to discern whether a character was uppercase or lowercase to handle both uppercase and lowercase encoding.</p>
</li>
<li><p>In lieu of using hard-coded values (sometimes referred to as &quot;magic numbers&quot;), literal character values such as 'A' and 'a' were used to clarify the process</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Performing a couple of quick tests, following was the terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Cipher_3/bin/Release$ ./Cipher_3 bcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyza
plaintext: hello
ciphertext: ifmmp
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Cipher_3/bin/Release$ ./Cipher_3 zabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxy
plaintext: ifmmp
ciphertext: hello
</code></pre>
<p>The takeaway from this would be to delve some more into C tutorials as it pertains to character variables, character arrays (strings), character functions, and string functions.</p>
